WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
Practical mitigations, layered.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:05.656
Most teams will not build a CaMeL interpreter this quarter.

3
00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:12.080
Here is a defensible stack for a real agent, roughly in order of how much protection each layer buys.

4
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.109
Break or gate the trifecta.

5
00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:16.779
Decide which leg this agent does not get.

6
00:00:17.059 --> 00:00:24.504
If it needs all three, gate outbound actions once untrusted content and private data have both entered the context.

7
00:00:24.784 --> 00:00:26.341
Allowlist destinations.

8
00:00:26.621 --> 00:00:28.669
Hosts, email domains, channels.

9
00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:32.533
A non-allowlisted destination is a deterministic deny.

10
00:00:32.813 --> 00:00:35.608
This is the control that survives encoding tricks.

11
00:00:35.888 --> 00:00:37.083
Track data flow.

12
00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:41.373
At minimum, session-level taint (has untrusted content been read?

13
00:00:41.653 --> 00:00:42.955
has private data?)

14
00:00:43.235 --> 00:00:46.285
Better, value-level provenance as in the exercise.

15
00:00:46.565 --> 00:00:49.125
Block private data in outbound arguments.

16
00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:51.005
Constrain the interfaces.

17
00:00:51.285 --> 00:01:04.128
Pass structured outputs between steps (enums, fixed fields, no free-form URLs), as OpenAI’s agent-safety guide recommends; strip or redact links and images in anything rendered.

18
00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:07.224
Keep untrusted text out of privileged positions.

19
00:01:07.504 --> 00:01:15.824
Never in the system or developer prompt; in tool results, labeled as data, as Anthropic’s and OpenAI’s guides both say.

20
00:01:16.104 --> 00:01:17.384
Use the model layer.

21
00:01:17.664 --> 00:01:24.043
A robust model, clear instructions that tool content is data, and an optional screening classifier.

22
00:01:24.323 --> 00:01:25.560
These lower the rate.

23
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.912
Approve the rest, rarely and with exact arguments.

24
00:01:29.192 --> 00:01:30.643
Test it adversarially.

25
00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:40.821
Keep an injection suite of fixture documents, run it on every change, and report two numbers together: attack success rate and task success on benign inputs.

26
00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:55.821
AgentDojo (Debenedetti and colleagues, 2024) measures exactly this pair, with 97 tasks and 629 security test cases, and found that both attacks and defenses leave a lot unsolved.

27
00:01:56.101 --> 00:02:00.581
None of these is sufficient alone, and you do not need all of them for every agent.

28
00:02:00.861 --> 00:02:03.720
Match the stack to the blast radius from lesson 10.
